@article{
  @meta{
    title: "Getting Started with Binary Search"
    author: "Jane Doe"
    tags: ["algorithms", "search", "tutorial"]
    difficulty: beginner
  }

  @content{
    @h1{Introduction to Binary Search}
    
    @p{
      Binary search is one of the most fundamental algorithms in computer science. 
      It's an efficient way to find a specific value in a **sorted** array or list.
    }

    @note{
      This article assumes you have basic knowledge of arrays and loops in programming.
    }

    @h2{How Binary Search Works}

    @p{
      The algorithm works by repeatedly dividing the search interval in half. 
      Here's the basic idea:
    }

    @list{
      type: ordered
      @@@
      - Start with the entire array
      - Compare the target value with the middle element
      - If they match, you've found it!
      - If target is less than middle, search the left half
      - If target is greater than middle, search the right half
      - Repeat until found or interval is empty
      @@@
    }

    @h2{Time Complexity}

    @p{
      Binary search has a time complexity of *O(log n)*, which is much better than 
      linear search's *O(n)*. This means it scales **extremely well** for large datasets.
    }

    @tip{
      Binary search only works on **sorted** data. If your data isn't sorted, 
      you'll need to sort it first (which costs O(n log n)) or use a different search method.
    }
  }
}
